      "reason": "Stop with the assessment of selection sensitivity. In model 1 of the 2.10 Å 2RE9 crystal structure, all nine fitted principal rotation angles are near 120°. Six of the nine prespecified comparisons exceed at least one bound (0.50° for the absolute difference in principal angle, 0.15 Å for the absolute difference in fitted RMSD). All six involve the ordered backbone. Keeping the same four backbone atom types and requiring occupancy 1 and B ≤ 40 Å² for each atom in all three chains reduces coverage from 164 to 49 residues. It lowers RMSD by 0.615 Å for B onto A, 1.205 Å for C onto A and 0.900 Å for C onto B. The corresponding absolute angle changes are 0.725°, 0.117° and 0.558°. Comparing common Cα with ordered backbone gives absolute angle and RMSD changes of (0.747°, 0.595 Å), (0.077°, 1.217 Å) and (0.622°, 0.940 Å) in the same pair order. Both contrasts exceed the RMSD bound for every pair and the angle bound for A–B and B–C. Classifications use unrounded measurements.",
      "round": 0,
      "stop_reason": "All three proposed atom selections have been tested and independently reproduced. The comparison at fixed residue coverage and the comparison at fixed atom types resolve the prespecified sensitivity question for the observed chains. One inventory job and three fit jobs used the four submissions, with no remaining protocol. A common-site evaluation of the different transforms would address how much of the RMSD decrease comes from changing the scoring sites. Recovering the effect of the 17 unobserved positions would require additional coordinate evidence."
